{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790355393,"nonce":"UCNzZ9dcdiGlM4S7i+YMUaOkNyY=","title":"Reloading a module leaves other modules' compiled code on a class's old layout","message":"reload_module invalidates only the reloaded module's own functions (tier.invalidate per FuncId it owns) and clears inline caches. Native bodies compiled in other modules stay installed, and they bake in facts about the reloaded module's classes:\n\n- getters and setters inlined as GetField/SetField at the old field indices;\n- class pointers in GuardClassAt (the ObjClass is reused across a reload, so these keep passing);\n- the address of the class's field-kind array, in LLVM kind-byte guards, in the Cranelift field-kind checks (196eca0), and in emit_note_field_kind_static.\n\n867669c keeps that array's address valid: a redefinition with the same field count ors the new bytes into the same array; one with a different count keeps the old array alive with every byte FIELD_OTHER, so kind checks against it fail and deopt. What stays wrong when the field count changes: old code still notes the kinds of its stores into the retired array, so the new array can say FIELD_NUM for a field that old code stores another kind into, and a new body's kind check then trusts it. Old code also reads and writes fields at indices that may now mean something else.\n\nFix direction: on reload, invalidate every native body whose compile inlined from, guarded on, or baked the kind array of a class the reload redefines — or all native bodies, since reload is a development-time path.","files":null}]}